{"year":"2024","content":[{"type":"text","content_text":"The 81st edition, on January 7, 2024, was the first after the end of the HFPA, now under the control of Dick Clark Productions and Eldridge, and marked the return of the live broadcast to CBS, which had not aired the show since 1982; the host was <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 114394, 'screen_type': person}\">Jo Koy</a>. <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 872585, 'screen_type': movie}\">Oppenheimer</a> (2023) was the big winner, with five awards, including Best Motion Picture – Drama and Director for <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 525, 'screen_type': person}\">Christopher Nolan</a>, and <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 792307, 'screen_type': movie}\">Poor Things</a> (2023) won Best Motion Picture – Musical or Comedy. The Cinematic and Box Office Achievement category debuted, won by <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 346698, 'screen_type': movie}\">Barbie</a> (2023), and <a href=\"https://_{type: 'screen', 'id': 1183917, 'screen_type': person}\">Lily Gladstone</a> became the first Indigenous performer to win a Golden Globe. <i>Television awards are not included.</i>"},{"type":"awards_nominees","name":"Best Motion Picture – Drama","nominee_list":[{"type":"movie","id":872585,"name":"Oppenheimer","image_path":"/dUPQszWoRSE9FucJTbVp2bwEi9G.jpg","director":"Christopher Nolan","winner":true,"backdrop_path":"/neeNHeXjMF5fXoCJRsOmkNGC7q.jpg"},{"type":"movie","id":915935,"name":"Anatomy of a Fall","image_path":"/woXYl0DJTx6TsfYWPkSfNHTsoOx.jpg","director":"Justine Triet","winner":false},{"type":"movie","id":466420,"name":"Killers of the Flower Moon","image_path":"/kBLMPD5BCmcrHKIhjZ86uWHZG5K.jpg","director":"Martin Scorsese","winner":false},{"type":"movie","id":523607,"name":"Maestro","image_path":"/spcH3yaPUtoSh0hNHikZ01D2OZ7.jpg","director":"Bradley Cooper","winner":false},{"type":"movie","id":666277,"name":"Past Lives","image_path":"/toSI71gFF11VnLfz2uiNx6jjNUF.jpg","direc